From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 21:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] llama: absorb the trailing ubatch (+31% prefill, +14% ttft,
 decode exactly neutral)

The ranked 512-token prompt tokenises to ~534, just over the default
`n_ubatch = 512`, so `llama_context::decode` splits it into 512 + 22. On a
256-expert top-8 MoE the cost of a ubatch is set by the routed expert weights it
must read, not by how many tokens it carries, so that 22-token tail pays a
**second full sweep of the expert weights for 4% of the tokens**. Measured with
`llama-bench -p 534 -n 0 -r 1` under `rocprofv3`: **6989 dispatches split,
3611 absorbed** — exactly the pp84/pp300 single-ubatch count — and pp534
**2711.17 -> 3349.56 t/s (+23.5%)**.

`llama_ubatch_effective()` runs the batch as ONE ubatch when it overhangs
`n_ubatch` by at most `min(n_batch, n_ubatch + n_ubatch/8)` (576 here). Both
`graph_reserve` sites are sized at the same bound, so the activation buffers grow
12.5% and nothing else changes. `LLAMA_UBATCH_ABSORB=0` restores stock and is the
A/B toggle below.

**This patch was structurally INERT until 0021.** The sibling
`laguna-s-2.1-gguf-gb10cuda-v1` shipped the identical change and recorded that it
never fires on the ranked path, because `llama-server` pre-chunks the prompt at
`{4 + n_ubatch, 4}` tokens from its end to create context checkpoints and
`llama_decode` therefore never sees more than `n_ubatch`. 0021 stops creating
those checkpoints, the server hands `llama_decode` the whole 534-token prompt,
and absorption can finally fire. The two patches compose; neither is worth this
on its own.

Measured on the trusted-runner box against the 0021 frontier, same binary with
the env toggle, **8 arms interleaved off/on/on/off/off/on/on/off**, ranked-harness
replica, 9 measured runs each:

| arm | TTFT | prefill tok/s | decode tok/s |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| off | 0.20588 | 4566.5 | 164.01 |
| on  | 0.18452 | 5851.1 | 163.81 |
| on  | 0.18400 | 5781.2 | 163.34 |
| off | 0.20967 | 4423.1 | 163.33 |
| off | 0.21054 | 4394.1 | 163.55 |
| on  | 0.18515 | 5814.1 | 163.49 |
| on  | 0.18553 | 5768.0 | 163.55 |
| off | 0.21063 | 4430.9 | 163.46 |

Medians **ttft 0.210105 -> 0.184835 (x1.1367), prefill 4427.0 -> 5797.7
(x1.3096), decode 163.505 -> 163.520 (x1.0001)**. Arms fully disjoint on ttft and
prefill; decode is neutral to four figures, which the mechanism requires since
decode touches one token at a time and never reaches the absorption bound.

The built-in control is the short arm of the slope: 84 tokens is one ubatch under
both arms, and it does not move (0.10817 -> 0.10704 s, ~1%). Everything the
prefill ratio gained came from `t(534)`.

Modelled score `1.0001^0.65 x 1.3096^0.20 x 1.1367^0.15` = **+7.6%**.

Correctness — and this change is batch-shape-dependent, so `-c 512` alone would
be worthless:

- **Runner's exact gate command** (`-c 512 --chunks 8`, no `-b`/`-ub`), 4 loads
  alternating: **5.2611 on every load, both arms** = stock. A 512-token context
  is one ubatch and never splits, so absorption provably cannot fire there and
  the reading has to be identical rather than merely close.
- **At a shape that does split** (`-c 534 -b 534 -ub 512`, ubatches 512 + 22),
  4 loads alternating: absorb=0 **5.3031 5.3031**, absorb=1 **5.3163 5.3163**,
  +0.249%. That number is not the patch's arithmetic. With the **unmodified
  b10237 binary** and only `-ub` changed:

  ```
  stock  -c 534 -b 534 -ub 512   (512 + 22)   5.3031   5.3031
  stock  -c 534 -b 534 -ub 534   (one ubatch) 5.3163   5.3163
  ```

  The candidate's absorbed reading **is stock's own single-ubatch reading, to
  every digit**. The patch selects between two batchings llama.cpp already
  performs; it computes nothing new.
- **The delta is a draw, not a bias**: at `--chunks 16` the sign flips —
  absorb=0 **4.3015**, absorb=1 **4.2906**, -0.253%. A random walk through the
  router's top-8, the same signature 0044 documented on the sibling qwen series.
- **Inert where it cannot fire**: at `-c 534 -b 534 -ub 534` the batch is already
  one ubatch and both arms read **5.3163**.
- **Served path**: 16 varied prompts x 64 greedy tokens through `llama-server`,
  both arms of the same binary, **16/16 byte-identical**, sampled-token logprobs
  agreeing at **max |dlogprob| = 0.000e+00**.
- Firing proven from the allocator, not a clock: `llama-bench -v` emits
  `n_tokens = 512` with absorption off and `n_tokens = 534` with it on.

Ported from `Sources/patches/laguna-s-2.1-gguf-gb10cuda-v1/0003`, which derived
the mechanism and the absorption bound.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/llama-context.cpp | 51 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 48 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/llama-context.cpp b/src/llama-context.cpp
index 19cca7d..748b9b2 100644
--- a/src/llama-context.cpp
+++ b/src/llama-context.cpp
@@ -574,6 +574,47 @@ void llama_context::resolve_fused_ops(const llama_memory_context_i * mctx, uint3
     }
 }
 
+// Adaptive tail-ubatch absorption.
+//
+// On a sparse-MoE model the cost of a ubatch is dominated by the routed expert
+// weights it must read, and that is a function of experts and layers rather
+// than of how many tokens the ubatch carries. A batch that overhangs n_ubatch
+// by a little therefore pays a SECOND full sweep of the expert weights for that
+// handful of tokens: on Laguna-S (256 experts, top-8, 47 layers) at the default
+// n_ubatch = 512, a 534-token batch splits 512 + 22 and the 22-token tail costs
+// 175.1 ms against the 512-token ubatch's 801.1 ms - 17.9% of the prefill for
+// 4.1% of the tokens.
+//
+// Returns the largest ubatch that may be formed by absorbing such an overhang,
+// or 0 when absorption is disabled. Rebalancing the split is NOT the remedy and
+// was measured: two 267-token ubatches pay two full sweeps.
+static uint32_t llama_ubatch_absorb_max(const llama_cparams & cparams) {
+    static const bool enabled = []() {
+        const char * env = getenv("LLAMA_UBATCH_ABSORB");
+        return env == nullptr || atoi(env) != 0;
+    }();
+
+    if (!enabled) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+
+    const uint32_t n_max = std::min(cparams.n_batch, cparams.n_ubatch + cparams.n_ubatch/8);
+
+    return n_max > cparams.n_ubatch ? n_max : 0;
+}
+
+// The ubatch size to split `n_tokens` with: n_ubatch normally, or the whole
+// batch when it overhangs n_ubatch by no more than the absorption bound.
+static uint32_t llama_ubatch_effective(const llama_cparams & cparams, uint32_t n_tokens) {
+    const uint32_t n_absorb = llama_ubatch_absorb_max(cparams);
+
+    if (n_absorb > cparams.n_ubatch && n_tokens > cparams.n_ubatch && n_tokens <= n_absorb) {
+        return n_tokens;
+    }
+
+    return cparams.n_ubatch;
+}
+
 void llama_context::sched_reserve() {
     if (!sched_need_reserve) {
         return;
@@ -588,7 +629,8 @@ void llama_context::sched_reserve() {
     const int64_t t_start_us = ggml_time_us();
 
     const uint32_t n_seqs = cparams.n_seq_max;
-    const uint32_t n_tokens = std::min(cparams.n_ctx, cparams.n_ubatch);
+    const uint32_t n_ubatch_res = std::max(cparams.n_ubatch, llama_ubatch_absorb_max(cparams));
+    const uint32_t n_tokens = std::min(cparams.n_ctx, n_ubatch_res);
 
     const size_t max_nodes = this->graph_max_nodes(n_tokens);
 
@@ -819,7 +861,8 @@ bool llama_context::memory_update(bool optimize) {
         }
 
         const uint32_t n_seqs = cparams.n_seq_max;
-        const uint32_t n_tokens = std::min(cparams.n_ctx, cparams.n_ubatch);
+        const uint32_t n_ubatch_res = std::max(cparams.n_ubatch, llama_ubatch_absorb_max(cparams));
+        const uint32_t n_tokens = std::min(cparams.n_ctx, n_ubatch_res);
 
         const uint32_t n_outputs_max = std::min(n_tokens, cparams.n_outputs_max);
 
@@ -1794,8 +1837,10 @@ int llama_context::decode(const llama_batch & batch_inp) {
 
     llama_memory_context_ptr mctx;
 
+    const uint32_t n_ubatch_eff = llama_ubatch_effective(cparams, balloc->get_n_tokens());
+
     while (true) {
-        mctx = memory->init_batch(*balloc, cparams.n_ubatch, output_all);
+        mctx = memory->init_batch(*balloc, n_ubatch_eff, output_all);
         if (!mctx) {
             return -2;
         }
